  "title": "Sixths, sevenths and the octave",
  "summary": "The wide intervals, up to the octave. After this you know all twelve.",
  "minutes": 6,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Sixths",
      "body": "Eight half steps make a **minor 6th** (C to A♭). Nine make a **major 6th** (C to A). Sixths sound sweet, and you hear them in a lot of harmony lines.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        68,
        69
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "C",
        "68": "minor 6th",
        "69": "major 6th"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Sevenths",
      "body": "Ten half steps make a **minor 7th** (C to B♭). Eleven make a **major 7th** (C to B), one half step short of the octave.\n\nThe minor 7th is the \"7\" in chords like G7. The major 7th is the dreamy sound in \"maj7\" chords.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        70,
        71
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "C",
        "70": "minor 7th",
        "71": "major 7th"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "The octave",
      "body": "Twelve half steps bring you back to the same letter: the **octave**. You now have a name for every distance from one half step to twelve.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        72
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "C",
        "72": "octave"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "Every interval above C",
      "body": "- C♯: minor 2nd\n- D: major 2nd\n- E♭: minor 3rd\n- E: major 3rd\n- F: perfect 4th\n- F♯: tritone\n- G: perfect 5th\n- A♭: minor 6th\n- A: major 6th\n- B♭: minor 7th\n- B: major 7th\n- C: octave",
